# Two-phase shear flow

This case follows the growth of a small wave on the interface between two
inviscid fluids when the upper, lighter fluid is sheared by a moving top
wall. The interface and velocity start from an unstable mode of linear theory
(wavenumber $k$, complex wave speed $c$), so the early growth can be checked against the theoretical growth
rate.

The code is also on GitHub:
<https://github.com/a-k-sangwan/Basilisk-Codes/tree/main/Shear-flow>.

## Files

- [main.c](main.c): the simulation.
- [genData.py](genData.py): computes the initial interface and velocity; `main.c` calls it.
- [output_vtu_foreach.h](output_vtu_foreach.h): VTU output for ParaView, from [acastillo's sandbox](/sandbox/acastillo/output_fields/output_vtu_foreach.h).

## Set-up

All quantities are in CGS units (cm, g, s).

| Parameter | Symbol in `main.c` | Value |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Wavenumber | `kK` | $k = 0.2921$ cm$^{-1}$ (wavelength 21.5 cm) |
| Initial amplitude | `aA = eEps/kK` | $a = 0.02/k = 0.0685$ cm |
| Complex wave speed | `cr`, `ci` | $c = 61.26 + 22.55\,i$ cm/s |
| Top-wall speed | `U0` | 199.57 cm/s |
| Shear-layer thickness in the upper fluid | `bB` | 2.54 cm |
| Density ratio (upper/lower) | `density_ratio` | 0.5 |
| Gravity | `gG` | 980 cm/s$^2$ |
| Surface tension | `f.sigma` | 72 dyn/cm |
| Viscosity | `mu1`, `mu2` | 0 (inviscid) |

The domain is a square of side one wavelength, centred on the interface,
and periodic in $x$. The lower fluid is at rest. The upper fluid has the
exponential profile $U(y) = U_0\,(1 - e^{-y/b})$, and the top wall moves at
$U_0$. The interface starts as $\eta = a\cos kx$, with the velocity of the
linear mode added to the base flow. Its amplitude should first grow like
$e^{k c_i t}$, with $k c_i = 6.59$ s$^{-1}$ (an e-folding time of 0.15 s).

The grid starts at $512^2$ cells, refined to level 10 (cell size 0.021 cm)
near the interface. It then adapts on the volume fraction, the interface
curvature and the velocity.

## Running

You need `python3` with numpy, scipy and sympy, and `genData.py` in the run
directory. `distance.h` and the VTU writer work only in serial, so compile
without `-fopenmp` and run without MPI:

~~~bash
qcc -O2 -Wall main.c -o run -lm
./run
~~~

To restart from a dump file, for example `dumpfile/dump-20` (the state at
$t = 20 \times$ `tdump` $= 0.1$ s):

~~~bash
./run -r 20
~~~

## Output

Every `tdump` = 0.005 s up to `t_final` = 2.5 s (501 times), the run writes:

- `dumpfile/dump-N`: restart file.
- `vtufile/snap-N.vtu`: volume fraction, pressure and velocity, for ParaView.
- `interface/int-N.dat`: interface segments from `output_facets()`, readable by gnuplot.

`track.out` logs the time, time step and wall-clock time at every step.

